Z||000004||rockcrawl||12-17-2000||08:58 PM||||terry,<BR> The 4.5 and 4.9 use sequintial port fuel injection with a centrally mounted throttle body. The 4.1 uses throttle body injection. Both systems look almost identical except for the injector rails and air cleaner.<P>Cozmo,<BR> Good find, but that ads $3843 to the otherwise inexpensive 4.9 swap. Actually more since I paid $350 for my used 4.9/4T60 and they charge $1700. I wonder if they (ASG) offer parts to those who want to build their own engine. The only parts they are replacing are the pistons, rods, and crank. The rest is just machine work.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||12-17-2000||09:20 PM||||Very interesting. I'll have to hack that.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||12-19-2000||02:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>Very interesting. I'll have to hack that.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No need. My father has figured out how to modify an HEI distributor to incorporate the optical pickup of an LT1 distributor. So far he has tested it hooked up to a an LT1 ECM and it makes spark. He has not yet tested it in a running car, however.<BR>     With this setup, anyone can use the very advanced '94+ LT1 electronics to control ANY small block chevy. We are currently prepping a TPI 400 to go into a 70's XJ6 Jaguar using LT1 electronics.<BR>     <A HREF="http://www.lt1.net/editor" TARGET=_blank>www.lt1.net/editor</A>  sells software to reprogram LT1 computers.<BR>     What do you know about LS1's?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||12-19-2000||06:22 PM||||There's always a need.....||||reg||1
Z||000008||Philphine||12-25-2000||02:13 PM||||the guy behind the pylon python just emailed me so i invited him too. <P>said he may do a kit also. <p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 12-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||rockcrawl||12-25-2000||10:08 PM||||Thinking about this, why not just use the ECM from a speed density 5 liter TPI engine? The displacement and flow characteristics of the engine are similar enough. I'm sure the F-body ECM has more performance oriented programming to begin with, and has already been hacked. Actually I think a mass-airflow system would perform better with fewer programming mods, but would require the addition of a MAF sensor in the intake path.<P>I haven't wired mine yet, so this will be something for me to consider. Aftermarket harnesses are available for the TPI that would make it really simple (but expensive). What do you guys think. Is it worth the effort? How much more power can you get from just changing the programming?<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||12-26-2000||04:21 PM||||I agree (mass air flow) would be best, but the F/Y body guys upgrade to the 90-92 Speed Density because the masses die too much and are costly.<P>Well, if some decides to run a 4.9L on the 7165 Mass/TPI or 7730 SD/TPI, let me know. I can help on the code part.||||reg||1
Z||000011||rockcrawl||12-26-2000||05:06 PM||||Terry, does the SD/TPI use sequential injection or batch fire? I know the MAF/TPI uses batch fire which would be a disadvantage as well as the addition of a $300 MAF sensor. I was also looking at the Accel fuel management system. For $850 you get the ECM, harness and all the sensors for either TPI or LT1. Performance can be precision tailored to the exact aplication using the optional Calmap calibration software ($193). It's made to work with a 350 or 383 though, no 305. I'm still trying to decide if it's worth the extra expense, or if I should just stick with the Cadillac controls. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||pherder||12-26-2000||07:20 PM||||rockcrawl,<P>You have another choice ... the Haltech E6GM computer.  It is an ECM that comes with the software to tune it using a PC (laptop, of course  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) <B>AND</B> the wiring pin outs are almost exactly the same as the V6 Fiero's!!!  Only a couple of changes and the wiring is done!!<P> <A HREF="http://www.haltech.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.haltech.com</A> <P>Cost is ~$700.<P>I have one for a TPI project that may or may not get off the ground.  I also have both the MAF and SD TPI ECMs and PROMs for my TPI project so I have several ways to go.<P>Paul<BR> ||||reg||4
